# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# pmt-frontend environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. Only *.example files are tracked; a real
# `.env.local` is gitignored and must never be committed.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is INLINED INTO THE BROWSER BUNDLE at build time. Never put a
# secret behind that prefix. Anything without it is server-only and is safe.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The backend API's origin, with no path.
#
# `lib/api/fetch.ts` appends `/api`, which is the backend's global prefix
# (pmt-backend/src/main.ts). NOT `/api/v1`: this app was copied from a dashboard
# whose backend was versioned, and every call 404'd until that segment came off.
# better-auth is mounted separately at `/api/auth/*` and does not go through
# that client.
#
# The ports are pinned, not incidental: backend 5050, this app 3000. The
# backend's CORS_ORIGINS must list this app's origin or every credentialed
# browser call CORS-fails.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL="http://localhost:5050"

# Must equal the backend's INTERNAL_API_SECRET exactly.
#
# Server-rendered fetches send it (`lib/server/auth-headers.ts`) so this trusted
# first-party origin bypasses the backend's per-IP NestJS throttle. Without the
# bypass, the app layout's fan-out plus a page's own request burst can trip the
# limiter; a throttled 429 then reads as "no session" and bounces a signed-in
# user back to the sign-in page, whose POST hits the same exhausted bucket.
#
# It exempts requests from the NestJS throttle ONLY. better-auth's own per-path
# limiter on `/api/auth/*` is a separate layer this key does not touch.
#
# Safe to leave empty for a single developer: the throttle then applies per IP,
# which one person will not trip. Set it once it starts firing during real work.
# Server-only, so it has NO `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix. Never give it one.
INTERNAL_API_SECRET=""

# The domain the session cookie is scoped to, read by `proxy.ts` ONLY when
# NODE_ENV is production.
#
# Deliberately unset in development: the cookie is host-only on localhost, and
# setting a domain here stops it being sent at all. In production it must match
# the backend's cookie configuration, or the guard's cookie-strip on a malformed
# cookie silently no-ops and the browser loops forever resending a broken value.
# COOKIE_DOMAIN=".example.com"
